<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-lagrange-multipliers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Lagrange Multipliers</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Recognize a constrained optimization problem: finding the maximum and
minimum values of a function <m>f</m> when its inputs are restricted by a
constraint of the form <m>g = 0</m>, such as a curve in the plane or a
surface in space.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain geometrically why, at a constrained extremum, the gradient
vectors <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\nabla g</m> must be collinear, and why
the level curve of <m>f</m> through the extremum is tangent to the
constraint curve there.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
State the method of Lagrange multipliers and use it to find the extrema
of a function subject to a constraint.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Solve shortest-distance problems with Lagrange multipliers, and compare
the method with the direct approach that uses the normal vector.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
Constrained optimizations are everywhere. A robot tracing a surface must
keep its hand on that surface, so its coordinates must satisfy the
equation of the surface at all times; planning the robot's motion is then
a problem of optimizing time, distance, or energy expense subject to that
constraint. Fitting a line to data points in an image can also be posed
as a constrained optimization problem. In
<xref ref="section-extreme-values"/> we found the extreme values of a
function <m>f(x,y)</m> over regions of the plane. In this section we
learn how to find the extrema of a function whose domain is restricted to
a subset of the plane such as a curve, namely the problem
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-problem" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\amp \text{maximize or minimize } f(x,y) \\
\amp \text{subject to the constraint } g(x,y) = 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
More generally, one can impose several constraints on a function of many
variables; here we work with a single constraint on a function of two or
three variables.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-method">
<title>The Method of Lagrange Multipliers</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-collinear">
<title>Why the Gradients Must Be Collinear</title>

<p>
The set of points satisfying the constraint <m>g(x,y) = 0</m> is a curve
<m>C</m> in the plane: it is precisely the level curve of <m>g</m> of
value <m>0</m>. We showed in
<xref ref="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> that the gradient of a
function is perpendicular to its level curves, so at every point of
<m>C</m> where <m>\nabla g \neq \mathbf 0</m>,
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-gradg-perp" number="yes">
\nabla g \cdot \hat{T} = 0,
</md>
where <m>\hat{T}</m> is a unit vector tangent to <m>C</m> at that point.
To stay on the curve, any small motion away from the point must be along
the tangent direction <m>\hat{T}</m>.
</p>

<p>
How does <m>f</m> change as we move along <m>C</m>? By
<xref ref="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/>, the rate of change of
<m>f</m> in the direction of the unit tangent vector <m>\hat{T}</m> is
the directional derivative
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-rate-along-C" number="yes">
D_{\hat{T}} f = \nabla f \cdot \hat{T}.
</md>
If <m>\nabla f \cdot \hat{T} \neq 0</m> at a point of <m>C</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-lagrange-tangent"/>, then the motion along the curve has a
component along <m>\nabla f</m>: the value of <m>f</m> increases as we
move along <m>C</m> in the direction of <m>\hat{T}</m> and decreases as
we move in the direction of <m>-\hat{T}</m>. Such a point cannot be a
constrained maximum or minimum.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-tangent">
<caption>
A point of the constraint curve <m>g(x,y)=0</m> that is not a constrained
extremum. The gradient <m>\nabla g</m> is perpendicular to the curve by
<xref ref="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve"/>, but <m>\nabla f</m> is not:
it has a nonzero component (dashed) along the tangent direction
<m>\hat{T}</m>, so <m>f</m> is still changing as we move along the
curve.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lagrange-tangent">
<diagram dimensions="(440,250)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t) = (1.4*t, 1.1*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.7,-1.7,3.7,2.3)">
<parametric-curve at="lt-C" function="c" domain="(-2.4,2.4)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="lt-ra1" endpoints="((1.100,0.781),(1.028,0.901))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="lt-ra2" endpoints="((1.028,0.901),(0.908,0.829))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="lt-proj" endpoints="((0.980,0.709),(1.623,1.095))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="lt-drop" endpoints="((1.185,1.823),(1.623,1.095))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" dash="3 4" thickness="1"/>
<vector at="lt-T" v="(0.943,0.567)" tail="(0.980,0.709)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="lt-gradg" v="(-0.515,0.857)" tail="(0.980,0.709)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="lt-gradf" v="(0.205,1.114)" tail="(0.980,0.709)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="lt-P" p="(0.980,0.709)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>P</m>
</point>
<label at="lt-C-label" anchor="(-2.3,-1.35)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
<m>g(x,y)=0</m>
</label>
<label at="lt-T-label" anchor="(2.05,1.28)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\hat{T}</m>
</label>
<label at="lt-gradg-label" anchor="(0.35,1.66)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\nabla g</m>
</label>
<label at="lt-gradf-label" anchor="(1.24,1.95)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A wavy green curve represents the constraint g of x y equals 0. At a point P on the curve, a blue arrow T is tangent to the curve, a green arrow, the gradient of g, is perpendicular to the curve, and a red arrow, the gradient of f, points in a different direction. A dashed red segment along the tangent shows the component of the gradient of f in the tangent direction. A small square marks the right angle between T and the gradient of g.">
  <annotation ref="lt-C" text="The constraint curve, where g of x y equals 0, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="lt-P" text="A point P on the constraint curve that is not a constrained extremum."/>
  <annotation ref="lt-T" text="The tangent vector T, drawn in blue: any motion that stays on the curve must be along T."/>
  <annotation ref="lt-gradg" text="The gradient of g, drawn in green, perpendicular to the constraint curve because the curve is a level curve of g."/>
  <annotation ref="lt-gradf" text="The gradient of f, drawn in red. It is not perpendicular to the curve at P."/>
  <annotation ref="lt-proj" text="The component of the gradient of f along the tangent direction, drawn as a dashed red segment. Because it is nonzero, f changes as we move along the curve, so P is not an extremum."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
At a constrained local maximum or minimum, then, a small motion along the
curve must not produce any change in <m>f</m>: the rate of change
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-rate-along-C"/> must vanish, so
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-gradf-perp" number="yes">
\nabla f \cdot \hat{T} = 0.
</md>
Now compare <xref ref="eq-lagrange-gradg-perp"/> and
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-gradf-perp"/>: at a constrained extremum, the
tangent vector <m>\hat{T}</m> is perpendicular to both gradients
<m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\nabla g</m>. In the plane, all vectors
perpendicular to the nonzero vector <m>\hat{T}</m> lie on a single
line, so <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\nabla g</m> must be collinear (parallel).
Phrased differently, there exists some number <m>\lambda \in \R</m> such
that
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-collinear" number="yes">
\nabla f = \lambda\, \nabla g.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-lagrange-level-curves"/> explains the condition
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-collinear"/> by superposing the constraint curve
<m>g(x,y) = 0</m> onto the family of level curves of <m>f(x,y)</m>, that
is, the collection of curves <m>f(x,y) = c</m>, where <m>c</m> is a real
number in the range of <m>f</m>. In the figure,
<m>c_1 \lt c^* \lt c_3 \lt c_4 \lt c_5</m>. Imagine a point moving along
the constraint curve from <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> to <m>(x_2,y_2)</m>.
Initially, the motion has a component along the negative gradient
direction <m>-\nabla f</m>, so the value of <m>f</m> decreases. This
component becomes smaller and smaller. When the moving point reaches
<m>(x^*,y^*)</m>, the motion is perpendicular to <m>\nabla f</m>. From
that point on, the motion has a component along the gradient direction
<m>\nabla f</m>, so the value of <m>f</m> increases. Thus at
<m>(x^*,y^*)</m> the function <m>f</m> achieves a local minimum on the
constraint curve, namely the value <m>c^*</m>. The motion is in the
tangential direction of the constraint curve, which is perpendicular to
<m>\nabla g</m>; therefore at <m>(x^*,y^*)</m> the two gradients
<m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\nabla g</m> are collinear, which is what
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-collinear"/> says. Since both curves are
perpendicular to the same line at <m>(x^*,y^*)</m>, the level curve
<m>f(x,y) = c^*</m> and the constraint curve <m>g(x,y) = 0</m> are
tangent at <m>(x^*,y^*)</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-level-curves">
<caption>
The constraint curve <m>g(x,y)=0</m> superposed on the family of level
curves of <m>f</m>, with <m>c_1 \lt c^* \lt c_3 \lt c_4 \lt c_5</m>. At
the constrained minimum <m>(x^*,y^*)</m>, the level curve
<m>f = c^*</m> is tangent to the constraint curve and the two gradients
are collinear: <m>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g</m>. Here they point in
opposite directions, so <m>\lambda \lt 0</m>; it is only the
collinearity that matters.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
