<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The x y plane with a green ellipse centered at the origin and several hyperbolas that are level curves of f equals x y. The red hyperbolas x y equals 2 and x y equals negative 2 are tangent to the ellipse at the four points 2 comma 1, negative 2 comma negative 1, negative 2 comma 1, and 2 comma negative 1. At each of the four points a red arrow shows the gradient of f and a green arrow shows the gradient of g, and in every case the two arrows are collinear. At the two maximum points, 2 comma 1 and negative 2 comma negative 1, they point in the same direction, so lambda is positive; at the two minimum points, negative 2 comma 1 and 2 comma negative 1, they point in opposite directions, so lambda is negative.">
  <annotation ref="ee-ellipse" text="The constraint ellipse, x squared over 8 plus y squared over 2 equals 1, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-h2-q1" text="The branch of the level curve x y equals 2 in the first quadrant, drawn in red, tangent to the ellipse at the point 2 comma 1, where f attains its maximum value 2."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-h2-q3" text="The branch of the level curve x y equals 2 in the third quadrant, drawn in red, tangent to the ellipse at the point negative 2 comma negative 1, where f also attains its maximum value 2."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-hm2-q2" text="The branch of the level curve x y equals negative 2 in the second quadrant, drawn in red, tangent to the ellipse at negative 2 comma 1, where f attains its minimum value negative 2."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-hm2-q4" text="The branch of the level curve x y equals negative 2 in the fourth quadrant, drawn in red, tangent to the ellipse at 2 comma negative 1, where f also attains its minimum value negative 2."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-h1-q1" text="The level curves x y equals 1 and x y equals negative 1, drawn in gray, which cross the ellipse instead of being tangent to it: the values 1 and negative 1 are attained but are not extreme values."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-max1" text="The point 2 comma 1, where the absolute maximum value 2 is attained."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradf" text="The gradient of f at the point 2 comma 1, drawn in red: it equals i plus 2 j."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradg" text="The gradient of g at the point 2 comma 1, drawn in green: it equals one half i plus j, so the gradient of f is exactly twice the gradient of g, and lambda equals 2. The two arrows are drawn slightly apart so both are visible."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradf-max2" text="The gradient of f at the point negative 2 comma negative 1, drawn in red: it equals negative i minus 2 j."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradg-max2" text="The gradient of g at the point negative 2 comma negative 1, drawn in green: it equals negative one half i minus j, so again the gradient of f is twice the gradient of g and lambda equals 2. The two arrows point the same way and are drawn slightly apart so both are visible."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradf-min1" text="The gradient of f at the point negative 2 comma 1, drawn in red: it equals i minus 2 j, pointing into the ellipse."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradg-min1" text="The gradient of g at the point negative 2 comma 1, drawn in green: it equals negative one half i plus j, pointing out of the ellipse. The two arrows lie along the same line but point in opposite directions, so the gradient of f is negative 2 times the gradient of g and lambda equals negative 2."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradf-min2" text="The gradient of f at the point 2 comma negative 1, drawn in red: it equals negative i plus 2 j, pointing into the ellipse."/>
  <annotation ref="ee-gradg-min2" text="The gradient of g at the point 2 comma negative 1, drawn in green: it equals one half i minus j, pointing out of the ellipse. Here too the two gradients are collinear and opposite, so lambda equals negative 2."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsubsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-example-distance">
<title>Shortest Distance to a Plane</title>

<p>
In <xref ref="example-shortest-distance"/> we found the shortest
distance from a point to a plane by substituting the equation of the
plane into the distance function and minimizing the resulting function
of two variables with the second derivative test. In the next example we
solve the same problem with Lagrange multipliers, and then once more
using the normal vector to the plane.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-lagrange-distance">
<title>Shortest Distance to a Plane, Revisited</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the shortest distance from the point <m>(1,0,-2)</m> to the plane
<m>x + 2y + z = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<p>
We find the closest point on the plane <m>x + 2y + z = 4</m> to the
point <m>(1,0,-2)</m>. The distance from a point <m>(x,y,z)</m> to
<m>(1,0,-2)</m> is
<m>\sqrt{(x-1)^2 + y^2 + (z+2)^2}</m>, and, as before, minimizing the
distance is the same as minimizing its square. So we minimize
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-f" number="yes">
f(x,y,z) = (x-1)^2 + y^2 + (z+2)^2
</md>
subject to the constraint
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-g" number="yes">
g(x,y,z) = x + 2y + z - 4 = 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
We compute the gradients:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= 2(x-1)\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath} + 2(z+2)\,\hat{k}</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla g \amp= \hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath} + \hat{k}.</mrow>
</md>
The Lagrange multiplier equation gives
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-system">
<mrow>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g \amp\;\Rightarrow\;
2(x-1)\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath} + 2(z+2)\,\hat{k}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \lambda\,\hat{\imath} + 2\lambda\,\hat{\jmath} + \lambda\,\hat{k}.</mrow>
</md>
so that
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-xyz" number="yes">
x = \frac{\lambda}{2} + 1, \qquad
y = \lambda, \qquad
z = \frac{\lambda}{2} - 2.
</md>
We substitute these into the equation of the constraint to find
<m>\lambda</m>:
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-lambda">
<mrow>g(x,y,z) = x + 2y + z - 4 \amp= 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Rightarrow\; 3\lambda - 5 = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; \lambda = \frac{5}{3}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore the closest point on the plane <m>x + 2y + z = 4</m> to the
point <m>(1,0,-2)</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-point" number="yes">
\left(\frac{11}{6},\; \frac{5}{3},\; -\frac{7}{6}\right)
</md>
and their distance is
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-distance-value" number="yes">
d = \sqrt{\left(\frac{11}{6} - 1\right)^2
+ \left(\frac{5}{3}\right)^2
+ \left(-\frac{7}{6} + 2\right)^2}
= \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{6},
</md>
in agreement with <xref ref="example-shortest-distance"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<solution>
<title>Another Solution, Using the Normal to the Plane</title>

<p>
To find the closest point on the plane <m>x + 2y + z = 4</m> to the
point <m>P_0(1,0,-2)</m>, we assume that the point <m>P(x,y,z)</m> on
the plane is the closest point to <m>P_0</m>. As
<xref ref="fig-lagrange-plane-prefigure"/> shows, the segment realizing
the shortest distance meets the plane perpendicularly, so the vector
<m>\overrightarrow{P_0 P}</m> must be parallel to the normal vector
<m>\mathbf n = \hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath} + \hat{k}</m> of the plane:
<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{P_0 P} = \langle x - 1,\; y,\; z + 2\rangle \amp= \lambda\,\langle 1, 2, 1\rangle</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-lagrange-normal-parallel" number="yes">\amp\;\Rightarrow\; x = \lambda + 1, \quad y = 2\lambda, \quad z = \lambda - 2.</mrow>
</md>
We then substitute <xref ref="eq-lagrange-normal-parallel"/> into the
equation of the plane:
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-normal-solve">
<mrow>(\lambda + 1) + 2(2\lambda) + (\lambda - 2) \amp= 4
\;\Rightarrow\; \lambda = \frac{5}{6}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Rightarrow\; x = \frac{11}{6}, \quad y = \frac{5}{3}, \quad z = -\frac{7}{6},</mrow>
</md>
and therefore the minimum distance is again
<m>d = \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{6}</m>.
</p>
<p>
The two solutions are closely related. At the closest point,
<m>\nabla f = 2\langle x-1,\, y,\, z+2\rangle = 2\,\overrightarrow{P_0 P}</m>,
while <m>\nabla g = \langle 1,2,1\rangle = \mathbf n</m>. So the
Lagrange condition <m>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g</m> says precisely
that <m>\overrightarrow{P_0 P}</m> is parallel to the normal <m>\mathbf n</m>: the
method of Lagrange multipliers rediscovers the geometry on its own.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-plane-prefigure">
<caption>
A schematic, edge-on view of the plane <m>x + 2y + z = 4</m> and the
point <m>P_0(1,0,-2)</m>. The shortest segment from <m>P_0</m> to the
plane is along the normal vector <m>\mathbf n</m>; the dashed segments,
which reach the plane in any other direction, are longer.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lagrange-plane">
<diagram dimensions="(450,290)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.0,-0.8,4.3,2.5)">
