</figure>

</subsubsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsubsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-n-variables">
<title>The Method in <m>n</m> Variables</title>

<p>
Nothing in the reasoning behind <xref ref="thm-lagrange-multipliers"/>
depended on the inputs being two or three numbers. The constraint
<m>g = 0</m> still describes a set of points on which we are allowed to
move; <m>\nabla g</m> is still perpendicular to that set; and at a
constrained extremum <m>f</m> still cannot be changing along any
direction we are allowed to move in, so <m>\nabla f</m> can have no
component tangent to the constraint set. As before, the only way for a
vector to be perpendicular to every allowed direction is for it to point
along <m>\nabla g</m>. So the method reads the same way for any number of
variables.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-lagrange-n-variables">
<title>The Method of Lagrange Multipliers in <m>n</m> Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be differentiable functions of the <m>n</m>
variables <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n</m>. To find the local maximum and
minimum values of <m>f</m> subject to the constraint
<m>g(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) = 0</m>, find the values of
<m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n</m> and <m>\lambda</m> that simultaneously
satisfy the equations
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-n-method" number="yes">
\nabla f = \lambda\, \nabla g
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
g(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) = 0 .
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Here <m>\nabla f = \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}, \frac{\partial
f}{\partial x_2}, \ldots, \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}\right)</m>, and
likewise for <m>\nabla g</m>. Reading
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-n-method"/> one component at a time turns it into
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_j} \amp= \lambda\,
\frac{\partial g}{\partial x_j}, \qquad j = 1, 2, \ldots, n,</mrow>
</md>
which together with <m>g = 0</m> is a system of <m>n+1</m> equations in
the <m>n+1</m> unknowns <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n, \lambda</m>. For
<m>n = 2</m> and <m>n = 3</m> this is exactly
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-method"/>. The bookkeeping grows, but the strategy
does not: eliminate <m>\lambda</m>, solve for the candidate points, and
then compare the values of <m>f</m> there.
</p>

<p>
One habit does need adjusting. In
<xref ref="example-lagrange-ellipse"/> and
<xref ref="example-lagrange-circle"/> the constraint curve was closed and
bounded, so the Extreme Value Theorem guaranteed in advance that the
absolute maximum and minimum existed and were among the candidate points.
In <m>n</m> variables a constraint set is often unbounded, and then no
such guarantee comes for free: after solving the system we must argue
separately that the point we found really is the extremum we are after.
The activity below does exactly that.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsubsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-hm-gm">
<title>An Inequality Between Two Means</title>

<p>
Our last application of the method is not an optimization problem at
first sight: it is an inequality, valid for any number of positive
numbers, that turns into a constrained minimum problem once it is
normalized correctly. For positive numbers
<m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n</m>, the <term>harmonic mean</term> and the
<term>geometric mean</term><fn>You are not expected to have met these
averages before, and nothing later in the course depends on them. The
<term>arithmetic mean</term> <m>A = \frac{x_1 + \cdots + x_n}{n}</m> is
the ordinary average. The <term>geometric mean</term>
<m>G = \sqrt[n]{x_1 \cdots x_n}</m> is the number that, repeated <m>n</m>
times, has the same <em>product</em>: for <m>n = 2</m> it is the side of
a square with the same area as an <m>a</m>-by-<m>b</m> rectangle, and it
is the right average for growth factors, since an investment that grows
by factors <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m> in successive years grows in total as
though it had grown by the constant factor <m>G</m> each year. The
<term>harmonic mean</term> <m>H = \frac{n}{1/x_1 + \cdots + 1/x_n}</m> is
the reciprocal of the average of the reciprocals, and it is the right
average for rates: drive one mile at <m>30</m> mph and the next at
<m>60</m> mph and your average speed over the two miles is the harmonic
mean <m>40</m> mph, not the arithmetic mean <m>45</m> mph.</fn> are
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-definitions" number="yes">
H = \frac{n}{\dfrac{1}{x_1} + \dfrac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \dfrac{1}{x_n}},
\qquad
G = \sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_n} .
</md>
The claim we shall prove is that <m>H \le G</m> always, with equality
exactly when all the numbers are the same.
</p>

<remark xml:id="remark-hm-gm-two-variables">
<title>For the Curious: the Two-Variable Case and a Picture</title>
<p>
This remark is optional. It is here for the reader who would like to see
what the inequality says before proving it, and none of it is needed for
the activity that follows or for anything later in the course.
</p>
<p>
For <m>n = 2</m> no calculus is needed at all. Write the two positive
numbers as <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. Then
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-two-var" number="yes">
H = \frac{2}{\frac1a + \frac1b} = \frac{2ab}{a+b},
\qquad
G = \sqrt{ab},
</md>
and since <m>a + b \gt 0</m> and <m>\sqrt{ab} \gt 0</m> we may multiply
and divide freely without reversing the inequality:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{2ab}{a+b} \le \sqrt{ab}
\amp\;\Longleftrightarrow\; 2ab \le \sqrt{ab}\,(a+b)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Longleftrightarrow\; 2\sqrt{ab} \le a + b</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Longleftrightarrow\; 0 \le a - 2\sqrt{ab} + b
= \left(\sqrt a - \sqrt b\,\right)^2 ,</mrow>
</md>
where the second line divides through by <m>\sqrt{ab} \gt 0</m>, using
<m>ab = \sqrt{ab}\cdot\sqrt{ab}</m>. The last line is true for every
choice of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, and it is an equality exactly when
<m>\sqrt a = \sqrt b</m>, that is, when <m>a = b</m>.
</p>
<p>
The middle line of that chain is worth a second look. It says
<m>\sqrt{ab} \le \frac{a+b}{2}</m>, so for two numbers the statement
<m>H \le G</m> is the very same statement as <m>G \le A</m>, where
<m>A</m> is the arithmetic mean. The two-variable case therefore carries
the whole chain
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-chain" number="yes">
H \le G \le A .
</md>
</p>
<p>
All three means can be read off a single picture, and the geometric mean
is the interesting one. It comes from a fact about right triangles: the
altitude drawn from the right angle to the hypotenuse is the geometric
mean of the two pieces into which it cuts the hypotenuse. In
<xref ref="fig-hm-gm-altitude"/> the right angle is at <m>C</m>, the
altitude <m>CD</m> has length <m>h</m>, and it splits the hypotenuse into
pieces of lengths <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. The two small triangles
<m>ADC</m> and <m>CDB</m> are both similar to the whole triangle
<m>ACB</m>, hence to each other, so matching the short leg of one against
the long leg of the other gives
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-altitude" number="yes">
\frac{a}{h} = \frac{h}{b}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
h^2 = ab
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
h = \sqrt{ab} = G .
</md>
</p>
<p>
Now lay the two lengths <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> end to end and draw the
semicircle having the resulting segment as its diameter, as in
<xref ref="fig-hm-gm-semicircle"/>. The radius is
<m>\frac{a+b}{2} = A</m>, drawn vertically at the center <m>O</m>. Any
triangle inscribed in a semicircle has a right angle at the point on the
arc, so the vertical segment <m>DC</m> erected where <m>a</m> meets
<m>b</m> is precisely the altitude of
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-altitude"/>: its length is <m>G = \sqrt{ab}</m>.
Finally drop a perpendicular from <m>D</m> to the radius <m>OC</m>,
meeting it at <m>M</m>. Applying <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-altitude"/> a
second time, now to the right triangle <m>ODC</m> with its right angle at
<m>D</m>, gives <m>DC^2 = CM \cdot CO</m>, so
<md>
CM = \frac{DC^2}{CO} = \frac{G^2}{A} = \frac{ab}{\frac{a+b}{2}}
   = \frac{2ab}{a+b} = H .
</md>
The chain <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-chain"/> is now visible rather than
computed: <m>CM \le CD</m> because a leg of the right triangle
<m>CMD</m> cannot exceed its hypotenuse, and <m>CD \le CO</m> for the
same reason in the right triangle <m>ODC</m>. Both become equalities
exactly when <m>D</m> coincides with <m>O</m>, that is, when
<m>a = b</m>.
</p>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="fig-hm-gm-altitude">
<caption>
The altitude from the right angle of a right triangle is the geometric
mean of the two pieces of the hypotenuse. Here the right angle is at
<m>C</m>, the altitude <m>CD</m> has length <m>h</m>, and it cuts the
hypotenuse into pieces of lengths <m>a = 4</m> and <m>b = 1</m>; the
similar triangles <m>ADC</m> and <m>CDB</m> give
<m>h = \sqrt{ab} = 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
