</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-hm-gm-semicircle">
<caption>
The three means of <m>a = 4</m> and <m>b = 1</m> in one picture. The
semicircle has the segment of length <m>a+b</m> as its diameter, so its
radius is the arithmetic mean <m>A = 2.5</m>, drawn in red at the center
<m>O</m>. The vertical segment <m>DC</m> erected where <m>a</m> meets
<m>b</m> is the altitude of the inscribed right triangle, so its length is
the geometric mean <m>G = 2</m>, drawn in blue. The perpendicular from
<m>D</m> to the radius <m>OC</m> cuts off the piece <m>CM</m>, whose
length is the harmonic mean <m>H = 1.6</m>, drawn in purple. Since a leg
of a right triangle is never longer than its hypotenuse,
<m>H \le G \le A</m>.
</caption>
