<annotation ref="figure" text="A semicircle drawn on a horizontal diameter of length a plus b, with a equal to 4 on the left and b equal to 1 on the right, meeting at the point D. The center of the semicircle is O. Three segments in the picture have lengths equal to the three means of a and b. A red vertical segment from O up to the top of the arc is the radius, whose length is the arithmetic mean 2.5. A blue vertical segment from D up to the point C on the arc is the altitude of the inscribed right triangle, whose length is the geometric mean 2. A purple segment along the radius O C, running from C back to the foot M of the perpendicular dropped from D, has length equal to the harmonic mean 1.6. Because a leg of a right triangle is never longer than its hypotenuse, the harmonic mean is at most the geometric mean, which is at most the arithmetic mean.">
