  <annotation ref="sc-diameter" text="The diameter, split at the point D into a left piece of length a equal to 4 and a right piece of length b equal to 1."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mean-a" text="The arithmetic mean, drawn in red: the vertical radius from the center O to the top of the arc, of length a plus b over 2, which is 2.5."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mean-g" text="The geometric mean, drawn in blue: the vertical segment from D up to the point C on the arc, of length the square root of a b, which is 2."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mean-h" text="The harmonic mean, drawn in purple: the piece of the radius O C running from C back to M, of length 2 a b over a plus b, which is 1.6."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-chord-left" text="The chord from the left end of the diameter to the point C on the arc, one leg of the inscribed right triangle, drawn in gray."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-chord-right" text="The chord from C to the right end of the diameter, the other leg of the inscribed right triangle, drawn in gray."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-right-c" text="The right angle marker at C: any triangle inscribed in a semicircle has a right angle at the point on the arc, which is why the blue segment is an altitude from a right angle and therefore the geometric mean."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-radius" text="The radius from the center O out to the point C on the arc. Its length is also the arithmetic mean 2.5, and the purple harmonic mean is the part of it nearest C."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-perp" text="The perpendicular dropped from D to the radius O C, meeting it at the point M."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-right-m" text="The right angle marker at M, showing that D M is perpendicular to the radius O C."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-lagrange-hm-gm">
<title>Harmonic Mean <m>\le</m> Geometric Mean, by Lagrange Multipliers</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Let <m>n \ge 2</m> and let <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n</m> be positive real
numbers, with <m>H</m> and <m>G</m> as in
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-definitions"/>. In this activity you will prove that
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-claim" number="yes">
\frac{n}{\dfrac{1}{x_1} + \dfrac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \dfrac{1}{x_n}}
\;\le\; \sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_n},
</md>
with equality if and only if <m>x_1 = x_2 = \cdots = x_n</m>.
</p>
<p>
The plan is the one described above: first use a scaling observation to
replace the inequality by a single constrained minimum problem, then let
<xref ref="thm-lagrange-n-variables"/> solve it, then check that the
solution really is a minimum. Throughout, write
<md>
P(x_1, \ldots, x_n) = x_1 x_2 \cdots x_n
</md>
for the product, so that <m>G = \sqrt[n]{P}</m>, and work only in the
region where every <m>x_j \gt 0</m>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-hm-gm-scaling">
<statement>
<p>
Show that for every constant <m>c \gt 0</m>, replacing each <m>x_j</m> by
<m>c\,x_j</m> multiplies both <m>H</m> and <m>G</m> by <m>c</m>. Use this
to explain why <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-claim"/> will follow once we know the
single statement
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-normalized" number="yes">
P(x_1, \ldots, x_n) \ge 1
\qquad\text{whenever}\qquad
\frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_n} = n
\text{ and every } x_j \gt 0 .
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Given arbitrary positive numbers, what happens if you divide every one of
them by their own harmonic mean?
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
Both means are <term>homogeneous of degree one</term>. For the harmonic
mean, each reciprocal is divided by <m>c</m>, so the whole denominator is
divided by <m>c</m>:
<md>
<mrow>H(cx_1, \ldots, cx_n)
\amp= \frac{n}{\dfrac{1}{cx_1} + \cdots + \dfrac{1}{cx_n}}
= \frac{n}{\dfrac{1}{c}\left(\dfrac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \dfrac{1}{x_n}\right)}
= c\,H(x_1, \ldots, x_n).</mrow>
</md>
For the geometric mean, each of the <m>n</m> factors picks up a
<m>c</m>, and the <m>n</m>-th root removes exactly one of them:
<md>
<mrow>G(cx_1, \ldots, cx_n) \amp= \sqrt[n]{(cx_1)(cx_2)\cdots(cx_n)}
= \sqrt[n]{c^n\,x_1x_2\cdots x_n} = c\,G(x_1, \ldots, x_n).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
So the inequality <m>H \le G</m> is unaffected by rescaling: it holds for
<m>(x_1, \ldots, x_n)</m> exactly when it holds for
<m>(cx_1, \ldots, cx_n)</m>. Now take any positive numbers
<m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m>, let <m>h = H(x_1, \ldots, x_n) \gt 0</m>, and
rescale by <m>c = 1/h</m>, that is, set <m>u_j = x_j/h</m>. Then
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{u_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{u_n}
\amp= h\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_n}\right)
= h \cdot \frac{n}{h} = n,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>(u_1, \ldots, u_n)</m> satisfies the constraint in
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-normalized"/>, and its harmonic mean is
<m>H(u_1,\ldots,u_n) = 1</m>. If we know
<m>P(u_1, \ldots, u_n) \ge 1</m>, then
<m>G(u_1, \ldots, u_n) = \sqrt[n]{P} \ge 1 = H(u_1, \ldots, u_n)</m>, and
multiplying both sides by <m>h</m> and using homogeneity returns
<m>G(x_1, \ldots, x_n) \ge H(x_1, \ldots, x_n)</m>, which is
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-claim"/>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-hm-gm-system">
<statement>
<p>
The statement <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-normalized"/> asks for the minimum of
<m>P</m> on a constraint set. Take
<md>
f(x_1, \ldots, x_n) = x_1x_2\cdots x_n,
\qquad
g(x_1, \ldots, x_n) = \frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots
+ \frac{1}{x_n} - n ,
</md>
both defined for <m>x_j \gt 0</m>, and write out the system
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-n-method"/> for this pair of functions.
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
When you differentiate a product of <m>n</m> factors with respect to one
of them, every other factor comes along for the ride. It is convenient to
write the answer as <m>P/x_j</m>.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
Differentiating <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x_j</m> leaves the product of
all the other factors, which is <m>P/x_j</m> since <m>x_j \neq 0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_j}
\amp= x_1 \cdots x_{j-1}x_{j+1}\cdots x_n = \frac{P}{x_j},
\qquad
\frac{\partial g}{\partial x_j} = -\frac{1}{x_j^2}.</mrow>
</md>
The method therefore asks for numbers <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n \gt 0</m> and
<m>\lambda</m> with
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-system" number="yes">
\frac{P}{x_j} = -\frac{\lambda}{x_j^2}
\quad\text{for } j = 1, 2, \ldots, n,
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_n} = n .
</md>
That is <m>n+1</m> equations in the <m>n+1</m> unknowns
<m>x_1, \ldots, x_n, \lambda</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-hm-gm-solve">
<statement>
<p>
Solve the system <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-system"/>. Show that it has exactly
one solution, and find the value of <m>P</m> there.
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Clear the denominators in the <m>j</m>-th equation by multiplying through
by <m>x_j^2</m>, and then compare the resulting equations for two
different values of <m>j</m>.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
Multiplying the <m>j</m>-th equation by <m>x_j^2 \gt 0</m> gives
<md xml:id="eq-hm-gm-cleared" number="yes">
P\,x_j = -\lambda, \qquad j = 1, 2, \ldots, n .
</md>
The right-hand side does not depend on <m>j</m>, and <m>P \gt 0</m>
because all the <m>x_j</m> are positive. Dividing by <m>P</m>,
<md>
x_1 = x_2 = \cdots = x_n = -\frac{\lambda}{P} .
</md>
So every candidate point has all its coordinates equal; call the common
value <m>a \gt 0</m>. (Incidentally, <m>\lambda = -Pa \lt 0</m>, which is
why the minus sign was needed.) Substituting into the constraint,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{a} + \cdots + \frac{1}{a} = \frac{n}{a} = n
\amp\;\Longrightarrow\; a = 1 .</mrow>
</md>
The only solution of the system is therefore
<m>(x_1, \ldots, x_n) = (1, 1, \ldots, 1)</m>, with
<m>\lambda = -1</m>, and there
<md>
P(1, 1, \ldots, 1) = 1 .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-hm-gm-minimum">
<statement>
<p>
The candidate is found, but nothing so far says it is a minimum: the
constraint set
<md>
S = \left\{ (x_1, \ldots, x_n) : x_j \gt 0 \text{ for all } j,
\ \frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_n} = n \right\}
</md>
is not bounded, since one coordinate may be made as large as we please.
Supply the missing argument in three steps.
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Show that every point of <m>S</m> satisfies <m>x_j \gt \frac{1}{n}</m>
for all <m>j</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Show that if in addition <m>P \le 1</m>, then
<m>x_j \le n^{\,n-1}</m> for all <m>j</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Conclude that <m>P</m> attains an absolute minimum on <m>S</m>, and that
this minimum value is <m>1</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
For (b), isolate one factor: <m>x_j = P \big/ \prod_{i \neq j} x_i</m>,
and bound the denominator from below using (a). For (c), the set carved
out by (a) and (b) is closed and bounded.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
<em>(a)</em> All <m>n</m> terms of the sum
<m>\frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_n} = n</m> are positive, and
<m>n \ge 2</m>, so each single term is strictly smaller than the whole
sum:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{x_j} \lt n \amp\;\Longrightarrow\; x_j \gt \frac{1}{n},
\qquad j = 1, 2, \ldots, n .</mrow>
</md>
In particular no point of <m>S</m> comes anywhere near the boundary
<m>x_j = 0</m> of the region where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are defined.
</p>
<p>
<em>(b)</em> Fix <m>j</m> and separate the <m>j</m>-th factor from the
rest of the product. By part (a) each of the other <m>n-1</m> factors
exceeds <m>\frac{1}{n}</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>x_j = \frac{P}{\displaystyle\prod_{i \neq j} x_i}
\le \frac{1}{\left(\dfrac{1}{n}\right)^{n-1}} = n^{\,n-1},</mrow>
</md>
using <m>P \le 1</m> in the numerator.
</p>
<p>
<em>(c)</em> Let
<m>S_1 = \{\, x \in S : P(x) \le 1 \,\}</m>. It is not empty, since
<m>(1, 1, \ldots, 1)</m> belongs to it. By (a) and (b) it is contained in
the box <m>\left[\frac{1}{n},\, n^{\,n-1}\right]^n</m>, so it is bounded,
and it is closed, being cut out of that box by the continuous conditions
<m>g = 0</m> and <m>P \le 1</m>. So <m>S_1</m> is closed and bounded, and
the Extreme Value Theorem applies to the continuous function <m>P</m> on
it: <m>P</m> attains an absolute minimum value <m>m \le 1</m> at some
point of <m>S_1</m>.
</p>
<p>
That value is the absolute minimum over all of <m>S</m>, not merely over
<m>S_1</m>, because every point of <m>S</m> outside <m>S_1</m> has
<m>P \gt 1 \ge m</m> by the definition of <m>S_1</m>. An absolute
minimum is in particular a local one, and
<m>\nabla g = \left(-\frac{1}{x_1^2}, \ldots, -\frac{1}{x_n^2}\right)</m>
is never the zero vector, so the minimizing point must satisfy the
Lagrange system <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-system"/>. By the previous task the
only such point is <m>(1, 1, \ldots, 1)</m>. Therefore the minimizer is
that point and <m>m = P(1, \ldots, 1) = 1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-hm-gm-conclude">
<statement>
<p>
Put the pieces together to prove <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-claim"/>, and show
that equality holds if and only if
<m>x_1 = x_2 = \cdots = x_n</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The previous task proves exactly the statement
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-normalized"/>: on the constraint set <m>S</m> the
product satisfies <m>P \ge 1</m>. By the first task this gives
<m>H \le G</m> for every choice of positive
<m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m>, which is <xref ref="eq-hm-gm-claim"/>.
</p>
<p>
For the equality case, suppose <m>H = G</m> and again put
<m>h = H(x_1, \ldots, x_n)</m> and <m>u_j = x_j/h</m>. Rescaling gives
<m>G(u_1, \ldots, u_n) = H(u_1, \ldots, u_n) = 1</m>, so
<m>P(u_1, \ldots, u_n) = 1</m>, meaning that
<m>(u_1, \ldots, u_n)</m> is a point of <m>S</m> at which <m>P</m>
attains its absolute minimum. By part (c) of the previous task the only
such point is <m>(1, 1, \ldots, 1)</m>, so <m>u_j = 1</m> for every
<m>j</m>, that is, <m>x_j = h</m> for every <m>j</m>: all the numbers are
equal.
</p>
<p>
Conversely, if <m>x_1 = \cdots = x_n = a</m>, then
<m>H = \dfrac{n}{n/a} = a</m> and <m>G = \sqrt[n]{a^n} = a</m>, so the
two means agree. This completes the proof.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

</activity>

<p>
One last observation costs nothing and doubles the yield. Apply
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-claim"/> not to the numbers <m>x_j</m> themselves but
to their reciprocals <m>y_j = 1/x_j</m>. The harmonic mean of the
<m>y_j</m> is <m>n \big/ \sum x_j = 1/A</m>, where <m>A</m> is the
arithmetic mean of the <m>x_j</m>, and the geometric mean of the
<m>y_j</m> is <m>1/G</m>. So <m>H(y) \le G(y)</m> reads
<m>\frac{1}{A} \le \frac{1}{G}</m>, that is,
<md xml:id="eq-gm-am" number="yes">
\sqrt[n]{x_1x_2\cdots x_n} \;\le\; \frac{x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_n}{n} ,
</md>
the celebrated inequality between the geometric and arithmetic means,
again with equality exactly when all the <m>x_j</m> are equal. Together
with the activity this establishes the full chain
<xref ref="eq-hm-gm-chain"/>, <m>H \le G \le A</m>, for any number of
positive numbers&#8212;the general form of what
<xref ref="fig-hm-gm-semicircle"/> shows for two.
</p>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

</section>
